WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.680
In this video, you export a virtual machine backup with Veeam Kasten on one SUSE Virtualization cluster, then import it on a second cluster and restore the virtual machine there.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:21.110
Kasten runs on the source cluster, harvester-ai. The namespace demo-k10dr holds a running virtual machine and its disk.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:26.140
Open the options menu of the namespace and select Create a Policy.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:34.800
The form fills in the name demo-k10dr-backup. For this demo, set the frequency to On Demand.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.740
Turn on Backups via Snapshot Exports, so the backup leaves the cluster.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.140
Open the export location profile list,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:48.330
and choose object-store, an S3 bucket that the second cluster can reach too.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:53.750
Submit the policy. Kasten creates it and generates the import data for other clusters.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.540
Open the actions of the new policy and select Run Once.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:09.380
Confirm. Kasten snapshots the disk and exports the snapshot and the virtual machine to the bucket.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:15.040
When the export is done, open the policy actions again and select View Import Data.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:24.320
The import token tells another Kasten instance where the exported data is and how to decrypt it. Copy it.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.450
Now switch to Kasten on the second cluster, far-edge. On the Policies page, select Create New Policy.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.720
Name the policy demo-k10dr-import,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.060
and choose the Import action instead of Snapshot.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.670
Run it on demand as well. A schedule would keep importing new restore points.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.420
Paste the import token into Config Data for Import.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:52.540
For the profile, choose demo-object-store, which points at the same bucket.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.130
Submit the import policy.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:02.480
Open its actions and select Run Once to import the restore point.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.200
Confirm the run.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.540
In Restore Points, the imported restore point of demo-k10dr is ready on far-edge.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.180
Open its actions and select Restore.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:24.660
Keep the namespace demo-k10dr. Kasten creates it on this cluster.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:31.530
The disk used the lvm-thin class, which far-edge doesn't have. Turn on transforms for the restored resources,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.210
and add a reference to an existing transform set.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:42.830
Pick demo-to-longhorn. It moves every restored volume to the harvester-longhorn storage class.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:50.970
In the artifact list, deselect the lvm-thin storage class itself. This cluster can't use it.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.430
Select Restore,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:03:00.940
and confirm. Kasten copies the disk data from the bucket and recreates the virtual machine.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:09.990
In SUSE Virtualization on far-edge, demo-k10dr is running, restored from the backup that harvester-ai exported.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:18.728
The virtual machine runs on the second cluster, restored from the exported backup in the shared bucket. Next, you run a DR plan with CloudCasa.
